WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.818
Imagine a neighbor asks where they
can get help using a computer.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.843
You may already have a familiar library,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.870
community center,
or veterans' organization in mind.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.190
Use that experience as a starting point,
then verify what is available now.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.750
Our task is to find a
suitable public service,

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.407
record enough evidence to revisit it,

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.717
and organize a fictional handout
without exposing private information.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.650
You can use a different local
service that matters to you.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.343
The names and details in this
demonstration are examples,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.013
not a claim about any
particular organization.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.090
A useful search ends with
a supported decision,

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.459
not just a page that looks impressive.

00:00:44.237 --> 00:00:46.569
Start with the words
that describe your task,

00:00:46.897 --> 00:00:48.866
the kind of service, and the location.

00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:55.117
Computer help library plus your town is
more useful than typing help by itself.

00:00:55.877 --> 00:00:59.597
Inspect the result title and the
website address before opening it.

00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:03.365
If the results are too broad,
add a specific service or location.

00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:05.512
If there are no useful results,

00:01:05.737 --> 00:01:09.416
remove one restriction rather than
assuming the service does not exist.

00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:12.870
Filters can help,
but they can also hide a relevant page.

00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:16.577
Pause and write a search for
your chosen community task.

00:01:16.577 --> 00:01:18.390
Explain what each word contributes.

00:01:18.697 --> 00:01:22.583
Then change one term and compare the
results instead of opening every link.

00:01:24.262 --> 00:01:27.942
A page can be accurate and still
be wrong for your decision.

00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:33.082
An official library page from another
town may describe a real service,

00:01:33.082 --> 00:01:35.310
but not the one available
to your neighbor.

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.724
Ask who published the information,

00:01:38.462 --> 00:01:39.683
what evidence supports it,

00:01:40.162 --> 00:01:44.522
when it was updated, why it was written,
and whether it answers your question.

00:01:45.462 --> 00:01:47.342
For opening hours or eligibility,

00:01:47.542 --> 00:01:52.522
go to the organization responsible for
that service and check the details there.

00:01:52.522 --> 00:01:54.962
Search-result snippets may be incomplete.

00:01:55.822 --> 00:01:57.522
Compare two pages that disagree.

00:01:58.182 --> 00:02:00.047
Do not count votes between websites;

00:02:00.702 --> 00:02:01.762
identify which source

00:02:01.762 --> 00:02:03.982
is in a position to know, and confirm

00:02:03.982 --> 00:02:07.682
uncertain details through an
independently found contact method.

00:02:09.396 --> 00:02:12.971
Suppose you find a useful service
today and need it again next month.

00:02:13.336 --> 00:02:15.776
A note saying good website
will not be enough.

00:02:16.276 --> 00:02:17.403
Record the organization,

00:02:17.656 --> 00:02:18.406
the page title,

00:02:18.616 --> 00:02:22.865
the web address, the date you checked it,
and the particular fact you plan to use.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:24.591
A bookmark helps you return,

00:02:24.996 --> 00:02:29.037
but it does not freeze the page or prove
that the information remains current.

00:02:29.376 --> 00:02:33.158
In your handout, distinguish a checked
fact from your own interpretation.

00:02:33.576 --> 00:02:35.597
If the page does not answer a question,

00:02:35.936 --> 00:02:39.896
write needs confirmation rather
than filling the gap with a guess.

00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:44.016
This is the digital equivalent of
writing the source beside a number

00:02:44.016 --> 00:02:45.143
in a household notebook.

00:02:47.007 --> 00:02:49.567
An online form is a structured request.

00:02:50.487 --> 00:02:53.147
Read its purpose and required
fields before typing.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.307
In our course practice form,

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:59.787
choose the kind of computer help needed
and a fictional contact preference.

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:03.347
Review the entries before
selecting the final button.

00:03:03.987 --> 00:03:04.867
On a real service,

00:03:05.307 --> 00:03:08.467
ask whether a sensitive detail
is necessary for the task,

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:11.227
and verify the site before entering it.

00:03:11.227 --> 00:03:13.707
Our practice does not send a real request.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.701
That distinction matters:

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:19.147
seeing a confirmation
message in a simulator proves

00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:20.805
that you completed the exercise,

00:03:21.197 --> 00:03:23.425
not that an organization
received anything.

00:03:24.087 --> 00:03:25.967
Pause, complete the fictional form,

00:03:26.487 --> 00:03:29.129
and explain what you
deliberately left out and why.

00:03:30.864 --> 00:03:34.584 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Think of a folder as a labeled
place to put related work.

00:03:35.264 --> 00:03:36.224 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Create a practice

00:03:36.224 --> 00:03:40.444 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
folder for the community resource
task and use a descriptive file name,

00:03:40.444 --> 00:03:44.444 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
such as library-help-draft.

00:03:44.444 --> 00:03:47.884 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A file saved on this computer may
not appear on another computer.

00:03:48.844 --> 00:03:51.544 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A cloud file belongs to
a service and an account,

00:03:52.024 --> 00:03:55.084 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
and a synced folder may keep
copies in more than one place.

00:03:55.904 --> 00:04:00.844 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Before moving to another device, check the
account and the saving or syncing status.

00:04:01.964 --> 00:04:04.284 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Reopen the file from
the intended location.

00:04:05.284 --> 00:04:07.524 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
That simple test catches a common mistake:

00:04:07.824 --> 00:04:12.084 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
editing one copy while later opening
an older copy somewhere else.

00:04:13.838 --> 00:04:18.718 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Our partner should suggest clearer wording
without rewriting the handout directly.

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:22.098 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Viewer access usually allows reading.

00:04:22.958 --> 00:04:27.298 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Commenter access, when supported,
allows suggestions through comments.

00:04:28.278 --> 00:04:31.018 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Editor access permits
changes to the content.

00:04:31.998 --> 00:04:34.578 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Choose the smallest
role that fits the task,

00:04:35.138 --> 00:04:36.796 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
inspect the recipient carefully,

00:04:37.398 --> 00:04:41.284 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
and have the partner verify that the
file opens with the expected controls.

00:04:42.538 --> 00:04:45.678 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A link that works for you may
still fail for someone else.

00:04:46.998 --> 00:04:48.398 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Also distinguish read-only

00:04:48.398 --> 00:04:49.118 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
from encryption.

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:51.578 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Read-only limits editing;

00:04:52.178 --> 00:04:56.323 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
it does not necessarily prevent someone
from reading or copying the information.

00:04:57.418 --> 00:05:01.798 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause and choose a role for someone who
only needs to read the finished handout.

00:05:02.698 --> 00:05:04.822 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Viewer is the appropriate starting point.

00:05:06.495 --> 00:05:07.455
Sync is convenient

00:05:07.455 --> 00:05:11.015
because a change can travel between
a device and an online service.

00:05:11.775 --> 00:05:13.951
The same convenience can carry a deletion.

00:05:14.655 --> 00:05:18.475
Watch the practice file disappear
from one location and then the other.

00:05:19.435 --> 00:05:22.075
That is why sync alone
is not a separate backup.

00:05:22.715 --> 00:05:26.175
A backup provides another recovery
copy under an appropriate plan.

00:05:26.175 --> 00:05:29.035
Version history may
preserve earlier content,

00:05:29.035 --> 00:05:33.095
while a recycle bin may retain a
deleted file for a limited time.

00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:36.675
Availability and retention
depend on the service.

00:05:37.395 --> 00:05:40.515
Do not test deletion with
your only important file.

00:05:41.375 --> 00:05:42.255
Use the fictional

00:05:42.255 --> 00:05:43.075
copy and explain

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:47.255
which protection would help if the mistake
spread through every synced location.

00:05:49.144 --> 00:05:50.644
First identify the problem.

00:05:51.244 --> 00:05:53.524
If the whole file is
missing after deletion,

00:05:53.924 --> 00:05:56.404
inspect the appropriate
trash or recycle bin.

00:05:56.924 --> 00:06:00.684
If the file exists but its wording
changed, inspect version history.

00:06:01.424 --> 00:06:05.673
Preview the earlier version and coordinate
with collaborators before restoring it.

00:06:06.084 --> 00:06:09.089
You do not want to replace
useful work someone just added.

00:06:09.504 --> 00:06:10.959
Compression is a separate tool:

00:06:11.364 --> 00:06:15.664
a ZIP file can package several files
for transfer and may reduce their size.

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:19.204
It is not automatically
encryption or a backup plan.

00:06:19.624 --> 00:06:24.004
After extracting a practice ZIP, open a
file to confirm the contents are usable.

00:06:24.744 --> 00:06:28.785
Choose the tool that matches the problem
rather than trusting a familiar icon.

00:06:30.679 --> 00:06:34.779
Pause and build a small resource
folder for your chosen community task.

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:38.699
Include a source note, a fictional
handout, and a clear file name.

00:06:39.079 --> 00:06:41.100
Tell a partner which fact you verified,

00:06:41.179 --> 00:06:45.239
why that source is appropriate,
and which permission fits the review task.

00:06:45.959 --> 00:06:47.359
Then explain how you would

00:06:47.359 --> 00:06:48.599
recover a deleted file

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:49.779
and how that differs

00:06:49.779 --> 00:06:50.999
from restoring earlier

00:06:50.999 --> 00:06:55.499
wording. If an account or sharing feature
is unavailable, demonstrate the decision

00:06:55.499 --> 00:06:58.219
with the course simulator
and label it simulated.

00:06:58.219 --> 00:07:02.139
For your next independent task,
reuse this sequence:

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:04.019
ask a focused question,

00:07:04.359 --> 00:07:09.219
check the evidence, record a trail,
organize the file, and verify access.
